%[text] ## Exercise 1: Sampling a One-Dimensional Harmonic Field
%[text] A simple scalar optical field can be modeled as
%[text]{"align":"center"} $E(x)=A\\cos(kx+\\phi), \\qquad k={2\\pi \\over \\lambda}.$
%[text] In this exercise you will sample the field on a one-dimensional grid and plot several phase-shifted versions of the same wave.
%[text] Tasks:
%[text] 1\. Use a wavelength of 633 nm, an amplitude of 1.2, and one phase angle. 
%[text] 2\. Convert the wavelength to micrometers before computing $k=2\\pi/\\lambda$ so it is compatible with `x\_um`. 
%[text] 3\. Create a column vector `x\_um` covering four wavelengths. 4. Create a row vector `phase\_family` containing at least three phases. 5. Use <u>element-by-element operations</u> and i<u>mplicit expansion</u> to create `E\_family`, where each column is one phase-shifted wave. 
%[text] 6 Plot the family of curves with labels and a legend.
%[text] Suggested variable names: `wavelength\_nm`, `wavelength\_um`, `amplitude`,`k`, `x\_um`, 'phases`,` E\_values\`.
%[text] ## Exercise 1 Work Area
%[text] TODO: Fill in your code for Exercise 1 here.
% Define the variables 
wavelength_nm = 633 ;
amplitude =  1.2 ;
% Define the number of points to sample the waveform
points = 801;
% Convert wavelength in nm to wavelength in micrometers
wavelength_um = wavelength_nm/1000  ;
% Define k
k = 2*pi/wavelength_um;
% Define x_um , phase_family and E_family
% Note:  make sure phase_family is a row vector and x_um is a column vector
x_um = linspace(0, 4*wavelength_um, points)';
phase_family = [0, pi/2, pi];
E_family = amplitude .* cos(k .* x_um + phase_family);

% Define a figure and plot
figure('Name','Solution 1: harmonic field') %[output:463b35f3]
plot(x_um,E_family,'LineWidth',1.3) %[output:463b35f3]
grid on %[output:463b35f3]
xlabel('x(\mu m)') %[output:463b35f3]
ylabel('E(x)') %[output:463b35f3]
title('Phase-shifted harmonic fields'); %[output:463b35f3]
legend(compose('\\phi = %.2f rad',phase_family),'Location','eastoutside') %[output:463b35f3]
